<mxCell id="e7" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;html=1;strokeWidth=2;strokeColor=#c62828;endArrow=classic;endFill=1" edge="1" parent="1" source="encoderSvc" target="s3"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
<mxCell id="e8" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;html=1;strokeWidth=3;strokeColor=#7b1fa2;endArrow=classic;endFill=1" edge="1" parent="1" source="heartbeatSvc" target="cassandra"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="why" value="WHY THESE CHOICES&#10;&#10;CDN-first architecture eliminates origin overload: 15M concurrent streams × 1 segment/6s = 2.5M requests/sec. S3 alone cannot handle this globally — regional S3 endpoints throttle at ~3500 PUT/GET per second per prefix. CDN (CloudFront with 200+ PoPs) absorbs 95% of traffic via edge caching. Cache hit: user in Mumbai gets segment from Mumbai PoP ~5ms. Cache miss (5%): CDN fetches from S3 Virginia ~200ms, caches it, serves all future requests from edge. Without CDN: all 2.5M req/sec hit S3 directly → S3 rate limits → users see infinite buffering globally.&#10;&#10;ABR (Adaptive Bitrate Streaming) prevents buffering: User's bandwidth varies every 10 seconds (WiFi signal strength, network congestion). Client measures download speed per segment, adjusts quality next segment. Slow internet (1 Mbps) → request 480p segment (600 KB). Fast internet (20 Mbps) → request 4K segment (8 MB). Video never stops playing. Alternative: single bitrate (e.g., always 1080p) → slow users buffer constantly, fast users waste bandwidth. HLS (Apple) and DASH (open standard) are the only battle-tested ABR protocols.&#10;&#10;Content ingestion pipeline automates transcoding: Backend team uploads one 100GB master file (ProRes 4444 uncompressed). Chunker splits into 720 × 10-second segments, creates manifest (.m3u8 HLS / .mpd DASH). Each chunk → Kafka → Encoder makes 4 quality versions (4K H.265, 1080p H.264, 720p H.264, 480p H.264). Total: 720 chunks × 4 qualities = 2880 encoded files per video. All stored in S3. Manifest URL saved in MongoDB. Without pipeline: manual transcoding per video takes days, error-prone, not scalable to 10,000 videos.&#10;&#10;MongoDB flexible schema handles heterogeneous content: Movie document has {duration, director}. Series document has {seasons: [{episodes: []}]}. Live event document has {scheduledAt, streamUrl}. MySQL rigid schema requires 5+ joined tables just to represent one series (seasons table, episodes table, foreign keys). Slow at reads. MongoDB stores each video as one self-contained JSON document — single-query fetch. Trade-off: MongoDB lacks ACID for multi-document transactions. Fine here because video metadata writes are rare (content team uploads once), no consistency risk.&#10;&#10;Cassandra handles 500K writes/sec for watch history: Every active stream sends heartbeat every 30 seconds (update watch position). 15M streams ÷ 30 = 500,000 writes per second. MySQL maxes out at ~50K writes/sec on enterprise hardware. Cassandra scales horizontally — add more nodes linearly increases write throughput. Partition key = profileId → all progress for one user on one node → single-partition reads fast. Clustering key = videoId + timestamp DESC → natural time ordering. TTL 90 days auto-expires old data. Alternative: MySQL sharded by userId — complex, requires application-level routing logic.&#10;&#10;Redis sub-millisecond cache prevents MongoDB overload: Play Service fetches manifest URL from MongoDB on every play request. 15M concurrent streams start at different times → peak 50K play requests/sec. MongoDB (even sharded) struggles at 50K reads/sec with 5ms latency. Redis in-memory cache: GET manifest_url:{videoId} returns in 0.1ms. Cache hit 99%. Only 1% misses hit MongoDB (500 reads/sec) → MongoDB comfortable. TTL 1 hour (manifest never changes, safe to cache). Without Redis: MongoDB becomes bottleneck, Play Service latency spikes to 50ms+, users see delayed playback start.&#10;&#10;Concurrent stream limits via Redis atomic INCR: Basic plan allows 1 stream, Premium allows 4. On play start: Redis INCR active_streams:{userId}. If returned count exceeds plan.maxStreams → reject with 429. On play stop: DECR. INCR/DECR are atomic — even if two clients start streams at exact same millisecond, Redis serializes the operations, one gets count=1, the other gets count=2. Without atomic operations: race condition allows users to exceed limits by starting 10 streams simultaneously (all read count=0, all increment to count=1 in parallel).&#10;&#10;Elasticsearch full-text search beats SQL LIKE: Search query 'action thriller' on 10,000 titles. MySQL LIKE query: SELECT * FROM videos WHERE title LIKE '%action%' OR genre LIKE '%thriller%' → full table scan, 500ms at 100K req/sec load. Elasticsearch inverted index: 'action' → [vid1, vid5, vid9], 'thriller' → [vid2, vid5, vid8] → intersection in 5ms. Also supports fuzzy matching (typo tolerance), ranking by score, filtering by releaseYear/rating. CDC pipeline (Debezium) streams MongoDB changes to Kafka → Elasticsearch consumer indexes automatically. Never query MongoDB for search (it has no inverted index).&#10;&#10;Kafka decouples payment from email: User pays → Stripe webhook → Payment Service writes to MySQL → publishes event subscription.created to Kafka. Two consumers: (1) Notification Service sends welcome email (may take 5 seconds if email server is slow), (2) User Service updates subscription_status=ACTIVE (50ms). Without Kafka: Payment Service would call both synchronously → slow email server blocks payment response for 5 seconds → terrible UX. Kafka makes it async, fire-and-forget. If Notification Service crashes, Kafka retries the event automatically when it recovers." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e3f2fd;strokeColor=#1976d2;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="840" width="1800" height="410" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

</root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>
